{
  "_title": "佛教术语的汉译 - Quinten Yearsley",
  "_description": "印度佛教词汇如何译成汉语：译者何时借音，何时译义，以及为什么。",

  "Translating Buddhist Terms into Chinese": "佛教术语的汉译",
  "How Indian Buddhist vocabulary was rendered into Chinese, and why.": "印度佛教词汇如何译成汉语，以及为什么。",
  "Buddhist Terms": "佛教词语",

  "Two strategies": "两种策略",
  "When Buddhism reached China around the 1st century CE, translators had a choice for every unfamiliar term: <strong>borrow the sound</strong> (音譯 <em>yīnyì</em>) or <strong>translate the meaning</strong> (義譯 <em>yìyì</em>). <em>Nirvāṇa</em> took the first path and became 涅槃 (<em>nièpán</em>) — characters chosen purely for their sound. <em>Śūnyatā</em> took the second and became 空 (<em>kōng</em>, \"empty\") — a character that already meant the right thing. Many terms ended up with both forms in circulation.": "佛教约在公元1世纪传入中国，译者对每一个陌生的词都要作出选择：<strong>借用读音</strong>（音譯 <em>yīnyì</em>），还是<strong>翻译意思</strong>（義譯 <em>yìyì</em>）。<em>Nirvāṇa</em>走了第一条路，成了涅槃（<em>nièpán</em>）——这两个字纯粹按音选取。<em>Śūnyatā</em>走了第二条路，成了空（<em>kōng</em>）——这个字本来就有正合适的意思。许多词最后是两种形式并行。",

  "Xuanzang's five categories not to translate": "玄奘的五种不翻",
  "The 7th-century monk-translator <strong>Xuánzàng</strong> (玄奘) — the same one who traveled to India and inspired the novel <em>Journey to the West</em> — codified the principles for when to transliterate rather than translate. His <strong>五種不翻</strong> (<em>wǔ zhǒng bù fān</em>, \"five categories not to translate\"):": "7世纪的译经僧<strong>玄奘</strong>（Xuánzàng）——就是西行取经、并启发了小说《西游记》的那位高僧——归纳出何时该音译、何时该意译。他的<strong>五種不翻</strong>（<em>wǔ zhǒng bù fān</em>，即五种不加翻译的情形）：",
  "<strong>Esoteric (秘密故)</strong> — mantras and dhāraṇīs, kept phonetic so their ritual power isn't lost.": "<strong>秘密故</strong>——真言和陀罗尼保持音译，以免失去其法力。",
  "<strong>Multivalent (多含故)</strong> — words with many meanings at once, like <em>bhagavān</em>, which combines \"lord,\" \"fortunate,\" and \"destroyer of evil.\"": "<strong>多含故</strong>——一个词同时含多义，如<em>bhagavān</em>，兼有“主宰”“吉祥”“破恶”之义。",
  "<strong>Absent referent (此無故)</strong> — things that don't exist in China, like the <em>jambū</em> tree.": "<strong>此無故</strong>——中国没有的事物，如<em>jambū</em>树。",
  "<strong>Following precedent (順古故)</strong> — terms with long-established transliterations, even when a translation would now be possible.": "<strong>順古故</strong>——音译早已沿用成习的词，即使今天可以意译。",
  "<strong>To inspire reverence (生善故)</strong> — keeping the foreign word when a translation would sound too mundane. <em>Prajñā</em> stayed as 般若 (<em>bōrě</em>) rather than the everyday 智慧 (<em>zhìhuì</em>, \"wisdom\") for exactly this reason.": "<strong>生善故</strong>——意译听起来太平常时，就保留外来词。<em>Prajñā</em>正因如此留作般若（<em>bōrě</em>），而没有用日常的智慧（<em>zhìhuì</em>）。",

