  "The translators": "译者",
  "Two figures shaped most of the surviving vocabulary:": "留传下来的词汇，大半出自两个人之手：",
  "<a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kumarajiva\"><strong>Kumārajīva</strong></a> (鳩摩羅什, 344–413) — translated the Lotus, Diamond, and Heart Sutras. Favored fluent Chinese over literal accuracy; most terms that \"feel\" Chinese (觀世音, 般若波羅蜜) come from his workshop.": "<a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kumarajiva\"><strong>鸠摩罗什</strong></a>（Kumārajīva，344–413）——译出《法华经》《金刚经》《心经》。他重视汉语的流畅，胜过字面的准确；那些读起来“像汉语”的词（觀世音、般若波羅蜜）多半出自他的译场。",
  "<a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Xuanzang\"><strong>Xuánzàng</strong></a> (玄奘, 602–664) — traveled overland to India for 17 years and produced famously precise translations. Where Kumārajīva smoothed, Xuanzang preferred to leave the Sanskrit visible.": "<a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Xuanzang\"><strong>玄奘</strong></a>（Xuánzàng，602–664）——陆路西行印度十七年，所出译本以精确著称。鸠摩罗什把文字磨平的地方，玄奘宁愿让梵语的痕迹留着。",
  "Earlier translators (2nd–4th centuries) also used <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Geyi\"><strong>géyì</strong></a> (格義, \"matching meanings\"), reaching for Daoist vocabulary to render Buddhist concepts — calling <em>nirvāṇa</em> 無為 (<em>wúwéi</em>), for instance. Most such renderings were later abandoned, but 道 for <em>mārga</em> stuck.": "更早的译者（2至4世纪）还用过<a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Geyi\"><strong>格義</strong></a>（<em>géyì</em>，即“比配意义”），拿道家的词来表达佛教概念——例如把<em>nirvāṇa</em>称作無為（<em>wúwéi</em>）。这类译法后来多被舍弃，只有<em>mārga</em>译作道留了下来。"
